From 73737e192750ce62ab0c998fe0fb2da365c108a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ayiejosh <132535860+ayiejosh@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 13:48:02 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 116/269] gallium/pvr: GL initialises on the driver - context,
 transfers, and the format gate

Three things, in the order the crashes demanded them:

1. pipe_context with buffer_map/texture_map/unmap and transfer_flush_region. The
   resources are already CPU mapped write-combined, so a transfer is arithmetic on
   that mapping: offset = box.y * stride + box.x * blocksize, with the block size
   used rather than assuming 4 bytes per pixel, which would read and write the
   wrong memory for the 16-bit formats. flush_resource is not needed; there is
   nothing to queue.

2. The full context contract. cso_unbind_context() calls every bind_* and set_*
   hook unconditionally on teardown, and st's throttle teardown calls the screen's
   fence_reference for each ring slot, so a NULL hook anywhere there is a crash on
   the way out of a context that was created successfully. These are stateless
   because there is no state yet - marked with the ceiling, because when the draw
   path lands they must record what was bound rather than silently ignoring it.

3. is_format_supported now answers for plain (non-compressed) formats. What it
   claims is narrow and worth being precise about: the driver can back such a
   resource with memory and describe its layout. It does not claim a draw path.
   Before this the state tracker built no configs, DRI had nothing to hand out and
   EGL could not initialise at all.

Result, on the real open kernel driver, no shim:

  OpenGL compatibility profile renderer: PowerVR B-Series BXM-4-64 (36.56.104.183)
  OpenGL compatibility profile version: 1.5 Mesa 26.3.0-devel
  OpenGL ES profile renderer: PowerVR B-Series BXM-4-64 (36.56.104.183)

GL now initialises on the native driver and reports it as the renderer, instead of
dying in st_init_extensions or on teardown. probe: ALL PASS, including a transfer
written into an 8x8 box at (4,4) and verified at byte 1040 of a stride-256 texture.

What this is NOT: the version is 1.5 / ES-CM 1.1, the level you get from zeroed
shader caps, so no shader stage exists and nothing can be drawn. There is still no
draw path, no rasterisation and no command submission, and no benchmark is possible
or claimed. Shader stages stay closed until pco is wired to something that draws.
